<body>
\1\n    <div id="site-header"></div>
    <!-- Header -->
    
    <main class="lesson-container py-4">
        <!-- Breadcrumb -->
        <div id="tutorial-header"></div>
        
        <div class="content lesson-content" style="font-size: 1.05rem; line-height: 1.7; color: var(--text-secondary);">

            <p>Troubleshooting VPNs requires isolating the failure domain: is the tunnel failing to build, or is traffic failing to traverse the built tunnel?</p>
            
            <h3>1. Identify the Symptom</h3>
            <ul>
                <li>Tunnel does not establish at all.</li>
                <li>Tunnel establishes, but no traffic flows.</li>
                <li>VPN connects, but local Internet breaks (common <a href="remote-access-vpn-explained.html">Remote Access</a> split-tunnel issue).</li>
                <li>Only specific subnets work (common <a href="site-to-site-vpn-explained.html">Site-to-Site</a> selector issue).</li>
            </ul>
            
            <h3>2. Verify Reachability</h3>
            <p>Can the client or peer router reach the external public IP of the VPN gateway? Check basic routing and ISP connectivity.</p>
            
            <h3>3. Phase 1: Authentication & Negotiation</h3>
            <p>If the tunnel fails to build, check IKE/IPsec or TLS session status. For Site-to-Site, this is almost always a <strong>proposal mismatch</strong> (different encryption/hash algorithms configured on each side), an incorrect peer IP, or a mismatched pre-shared key/certificate. For Remote Access, verify user credentials, MFA prompts, and endpoint posture checks.</p>
            
            <h3>4. Phase 2: Selectors and Subnets</h3>
            <p>If the tunnel builds but traffic fails, verify the "selectors" (the interesting traffic). In policy-based Site-to-Site VPNs, the local subnet configuration on Router A must perfectly mirror the remote subnet configuration on Router B. Overlapping subnets (where both sides use 192.168.1.0/24) will cause immediate routing failures.</p>
            
            <h3>5. Routing and NAT</h3>
            <p>Does the firewall know to route the internal traffic into the tunnel? Is NAT (Network Address Translation) improperly translating the private IPs before they enter the tunnel? A "NAT Exemption" rule is often required to prevent this.</p>
            
            <h3>6. Firewall Policy and MTU</h3>
            <p>VPN traffic is usually subject to <a href="stateful-firewall-explained.html">stateful firewall</a> inspection. Ensure a policy permits the decrypted traffic. Finally, if small pings work but file transfers fail, you likely have an <a href="mtu-and-mss-explained.html">MTU/MSS</a> fragmentation issue due to the VPN encapsulation overhead.</p>
        
        </div>
        
